artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an infrared tip-tilt sensor (TTS) that observes a guide star through an adaptive-optics (AO) system providing only partial wavefront correction, so that the resulting point-spread function (PSF) on the TTS detector has a low Strehl ratio (below roughly 10%). For an off-axis guide star, anisoplanatism broadens the PSF and the diffraction-limited core may break up into two or more bright speckles whose positions and relative intensities vary in time. The TTS measurement model is: at each time step t, the detector records pixel intensities I_t(i,j) = S_t(i,j; x_t, y_t) + n_t(i,j), where S_t is the (unknown, time-varying) low-Strehl PSF whose centroid position (x_t, y_t) on the detector encodes the instantaneous tip-tilt of the wavefront, and n_t is detector read noise plus shot noise. The total read-noise variance contributed to a centroid estimate grows with the number of pixels read out, and the variance of a standard center-of-gravity estimator also grows with the size of the readout window even at fixed per-pixel noise. A peak-finding/correlation estimator that locks onto the brightest speckle yields biased tip-tilt estimates because the dominant speckle migrates between distinct positions over time.

Problem. Design a spot-displacement estimator (x̂_t, ŷ_t) of the underlying tip-tilt position together with a closed-loop control law for the AO tip-tilt mirror, operating on the sequence {I_t}, such that:

(i) the estimator is unbiased (or has provably bounded bias) under speckle reorganization in the low-Strehl, anisoplanatic regime;
(ii) its variance is smaller than that of the windowed center-of-gravity estimator that would be required to capture the broadened spot;
(iii) it does not reduce, in the limit of multi-speckle PSFs, to tracking the single brightest speckle;
(iv) the resulting closed loop is stable and yields accurate tip-tilt correction on sky.

Characterize the optimal such estimator and control law, or prove fundamental lower bounds (e.g., a Cramér–Rao-type bound) on the achievable tip-tilt estimation error in this regime.

